# Claude Code workflow reference

Env-specific facts and decisions only, each at its current state with a dated
annotation. Generic harness behavior (commands, flags, modes) is injected into
every session or answerable live via the claude-code-guide agent — it does not
belong here; sweep measurements and audit baselines live in the chezmoi repo's
`docs/agents-audit-log.md`, not here. If behavior seems changed, verify with
that agent and update this file.

## Models (last verified 2026-09-15)

- Global model is pinned in settings (`model` key) from the chezmoi repo as
  `{{ .agents.claude.defaults.model }}`; `/model` changes only the current
  session. The pin is the frontier tier as the session driver (decision
  2026-09-15, research record in the audit log); `[1m]` holds the 1M-context
  variant — without it Fable 5 runs at 200K (probed and doc-checked
  2026-09-15). Fable 5, not 5.1: the stable channel sits below the 5.1 floor
  (2.1.255 per Anthropic's help center) — bump the pin and
  `subagent_tiers.claude.frontier` together once stable clears it. Driver
  effort is the Claude Code default; Anthropic's "low-effort Fable beats Opus
  on cost per task" claim is API pricing, not Max-plan metering — an open
  lever, measured before changed.
- Pins use explicit model IDs, never bare aliases: aliases re-resolve across
  CLI releases (`opus`/`sonnet` float to the newest generation at v2.1.219+,
  and `haiku` was observed resolving to sonnet-5), so an alias pin can silently
  change tier. The agent-instructions-audit skill live-probes every pin.
- Subagent tiers are relative capability, not fixed model names; the named
  specialists are pinned per tier in `.chezmoidata/agents.yaml`
  (`subagent_tiers.claude`) and do NOT follow `/model`. Unpinned built-ins
  (general-purpose, Plan, claude, claude-code-guide) take
  `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_MODEL`, set in settings `env` to the top worker tier
  (`{{ .subagent_tiers.claude.top }}`), so nothing inherits the driver by
  omission; a subagent naming an unavailable model silently falls back. Forks
  (`subagent_type: fork`) always run the parent model — a fork is a
  driver-context continuation, not a worker. Tier routing rules live in
  CLAUDE.md (lowest tier likely to one-shot the task).
- Subagent model resolution, in order (re-verified 2026-09-15 against
  `code.claude.com/docs/en/sub-agents`, v2.1.251+): the per-invocation
  `model` parameter, then the definition's `model` frontmatter (where
  `inherit` selects the main conversation's model), then
  `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_MODEL`, then the main conversation's model. Omitting
  `model:` is therefore NOT equivalent to `model: inherit` — omission lets the
  env var win, which is now the intended path; no roster entry uses `inherit`
  any more, since under a frontier driver it would mean "silently consume
  frontier". `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_MODEL_FORCE` (v2.1.257+) is deliberately
  unset: it would also erase the specialists' pins.
- No child runs the frontier tier: `~/.claude/hooks/deny-frontier-child.mjs`
  (PreToolUse, matcher `Agent`, rendered from agents.yaml) denies a per-call
  `model` of the frontier pin, `fable` or `inherit`, inside subagents too
  (user-level hooks apply there); forks carry no `model` and run the parent
  by harness design. Residual the hook cannot see: a third-party (plugin or
  project) agent definition declaring `model: inherit`, invoked without a
  per-call model, resolves to the driver — the roster here carries none, and
  the transcript sweep's children-vs-pins count is the check. The silent upstream failure modes — claude-code#75054 (pins lost on
  background resume), #85592 (env var overriding per-call requests at
  2.1.223), #82252 (an override served by another model while the child
  self-reports the requested one) — are not hook-catchable: after each CLI
  update dispatch one unpinned child and one with an explicit
  `{{ .subagent_tiers.claude.top }}` override and read `message.model` in the
  transcript, never the child's self-report.
- Subscription limits (Max plan; re-checked 2026-09-14 against the Fable
  support article): a rolling 5-hour session window plus a shared weekly pool,
  "shared across all models"; Fable draws the same pool, capped at up to 50%
  of weekly usage and "using limits faster than other Claude models" (exact
  weighting unpublished; the earlier free-Fable-5 promo ended 2026-07-19 and
  never covered 5.1). The errors doc still names Opus and Sonnet family
  sub-limit messages, an unresolved contradiction — next time a limit binds,
  reconcile against `/usage`.
- Delegation economics (researched 2026-09-12; metering re-checked
  2026-09-14; frontier-driver framing 2026-09-15; baselines in the audit
  log): subagent tokens draw the same Max 5h/weekly pools as the main loop —
  no subagent discount — and drain is model-weighted, so the driver's own
  context is the expensive one: it re-meters at Fable's weight every turn
  and cache reads count non-negligibly against the pool (measured via
  claude-code#91220). Delegation multiplies total tokens and pays only via
  tier arbitrage and keeping that context lean — delegate bounded
  implementation, reads, research and review, keep trivial edits and
  tightly sequential steps inline, and treat session length as the primary
  cost lever; exact cache-read weighting still unpublished. Bucket caveat:
  exhausting a cheaper model's sub-limit can force frontier fallback for
  routine work (claude-code#55663).
- `implementer` (added 2026-08-28): mid-tier executor for spec-complete
  mechanical write work — a bounded edit delegated out of the driver's
  context avoids re-sending the whole context at frontier rates. The
  2026-09-11/12 sweep found it undispatched while every edit ran inline in
  Fable sessions; its dispatch count is an open audit trigger.
- `spec-reviewer` runs at the top worker tier (`{{ .subagent_tiers.claude.top }}`),
  not `inherit` (decision 2026-09-15): the reviewer only proposes, and the
  evidence behind the old reviewer-≥-producer floor was a cross-vendor
  reviewer that rewrote the program. Known limit: Opus 5 recall is weakest on
  concurrency and subtle logic defects (CodeRabbit 2026-07-24), which
  `codex-review` covers on high-stakes surfaces; high/medium catches at the
  new tier are an open audit trigger against the Fable baseline.
- `researcher` (added 2026-09-08): scoped mid-tier read-only preset with web,
  Exa and context7, so research dispatches stop hand-picking models on
  general-purpose.
- Codex pin caveat for pinned reviewers: openai/codex#32587 (still open as of
  2026-09-15) — a spawned Codex child can silently inherit the parent's
  model/effort instead of its pinned config, which under an Astra parent
  costs 2.5x; verify via `/status` after each Codex CLI update.
- If Codex is available, `codex-review` / `codex-advisor` are read-only Codex
  consultation for high-risk diffs or stuck calls; Claude and Codex meter on
  independent windows, so read-work can offload to Codex — write-work means driving
  the task with Codex yourself. Keep decision ownership in Claude. The bridge
  is pinned to `{{ .subagent_tiers.codex.top }}` (`--model`, anchored in
  agents.yaml) while interactive Codex drives on Astra: review is a worker
  role, no source shows Astra reviewing better, and Astra is 2.5x the credits
  and ~3x slower to first token on a call that blocks the Claude loop
  (research 2026-09-15).
- The codex-review trigger is risk-or-breadth, never blanket (decision
  2026-09-03, kept 2026-09-08): a size-based trigger produced ~2/3
  zero-finding reviews while every surviving catch sat in a high-stakes
  category. Yield is findings the session actually fixed; the
  survived-findings count accumulates in the audit log — decide the trigger's
  fate once there are ten.

## Codex transport: owned MCP bridge (decision 2026-09-05)

- `codex mcp-server` (the previous transport) was deprecated in codex-cli
  0.149.1 with removal announced, so the `codex` MCP entry now spawns
  `scripts/codex-bridge.mjs` from the chezmoi source repo, wrapping the stable
  `codex exec` / `codex exec review` CLI (verified present at 0.153.1). Tools:
  `review`, `advise`, `reply`; invocation fixed to read-only sandbox, never
  approvals, high reasoning — callers pick scope only, never sandbox flags.
  `review` states the scope inside the prompt when custom instructions are
  given: the CLI rejects a PROMPT combined with `--base`/`--uncommitted`
  (verified at 0.153.4).
- MCP transport kept deliberately: the bridge is harness-spawned outside the
  Bash sandbox (the `~/.codex` deny stays fully intact), and its rendered
  allow rule (`mcp__codex`, from `auto_approve_tools`) makes the tools
  prompt-free in plan mode — verified live 2026-09-05. Plan mode blocks MCP
  tools as a class unless allow-listed (`readOnlyHint` is ignored; upstream
  issue #12368 closed "not planned"), and Bash has no plan-mode allowlist
  exemption at all, which rules out every Bash-based transport (plain
  `codex exec` calls and the plugin alike).

## Codex plugin (not installed — reaffirmed 2026-09-05)

- OpenAI's official plugin (`openai/codex-plugin-cc`, first dissected at
  v1.0.6) stays uninstalled, on updated grounds: its command paths spawn the
  experimental `codex app-server` through sandboxed Bash — still blocked by
  the `~/.codex` deny, never prompt-free in plan mode (see above), and riding
  a protocol OpenAI documents as changeable without notice. The bridge covers
  review + advice on the stable `exec` interface with no sandbox carve-out.
  The extras remain excluded by doctrine (write-capable rescue, stop-review
  gate, session transfer, background job manager). Reconsider only if the
  `codex exec` interface itself breaks AND the sandbox/plan-mode constraints
  change.

## pi (side-by-side trial vs codex, started 2026-09-05)

- pi (`earendil-works/pi`, adopted at v0.85.0) runs interactive GPT sessions
  alongside codex until one wins the trial; ChatGPT-subscription OAuth in
  third-party OSS harnesses is OpenAI-sanctioned (Codex for OSS program names
  pi; last verified 2026-09-05) and draws the same plan quota as codex.
- pi ships no permission system: the managed workflow extension leases a
  per-tool srt (`@anthropic-ai/sandbox-runtime`) worker for every
  `workspace_*` call, its profile rendered from `.chezmoidata/agents.yaml`,
  and the `pi` zsh function launches the pinned CLI with that extension; the
  workflow's own plan mode supplies read-only planning (verified 2026-09-09).
  `~/.pi/agent/settings.json` is key-merged by a `modify_` script (since
  2026-09-06): the managed subset renders from agents.yaml while pi's own
  trust and UI state survives every apply.
- Trial decision trigger: revisit at the next `agent-instructions-audit`, or
  sooner once one harness clearly owns all interactive GPT sessions — then
  demote or drop the loser (codex keeps the review-backend role regardless).
  Still not decidable as of the 2026-09-12 audit (status history in the audit
  log).
